{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== OSS / ECOSYSTEM RESEARCH, BY OPERATOR DIRECTION: IS MIRRORING A PEER-SIDE LINK DELETION A PARITY GAP? ===\n\nThe operator's decision rule: \"How do popular and actively maintained OSS projects with Jira integration\nhandle this? If this is a parity gap, build it now. If we would be implementing something that's not\ncommonly handled, defer it.\"\n\n**FINDING: it is NOT commonly handled. Baseline behaviour across the field is additive / one-way for\nRELATIONSHIP deletions, and the tools that deliberately refuse it cite exactly the risk we just lived\nthrough.**\n\nEvidence gathered:\n\n * **Aha!** states the design decision outright: it does not delete records based on updates from\n   integrations, \"because there is too much risk of users inadvertently deleting data, especially when\n   they are just getting started.\"\n   https://big.ideas.aha.io/ideas/A-I-8761\n * **Asana <-> Jira** (first-party, actively maintained bidirectional sync): when an item is deleted in\n   one tool it STILL EXISTS in the other, merely without the connection. Deletion does not propagate.\n   https://forum.asana.com/t/jira-integration-deleting-task-still-exists/1030319\n * **Adobe Workfront <-> Jira**: deleting on the Workfront side does not delete or deactivate the linked\n   Jira item; users report it and it is answered as expected behaviour, not a bug.\n   https://experienceleaguecommunities.adobe.com/adobe-workfront-23/jira-integrations-when-deleting-something-from-workfront-it-doesn-t-push-to-jira-is-the-config-not-set-up-correctly-or-is-this-a-know-bug-still-shows-as-active-task-issue-in-jira-120914\n * **Atlassian's OWN first-party tooling is unreliable here**: there is a support KB for \"the Delete\n   Issue Links action does not delete any link\" in Automation for Jira — i.e. even Atlassian's native\n   link-deletion primitive has a documented failure mode.\n   https://support.atlassian.com/automation/kb/delete-issue-links-action-does-not-delete-any-link/\n * **Exalate** — the most capable tool in this space, and the one that CAN mirror links — treats issue-link\n   sync as an ADVANCED, OPT-IN, SCRIPTED behaviour, not a default: link sync must be explicitly\n   configured, every linked issue must be synchronised separately to preserve the association, and\n   support advises manually DISCONNECTING a pair BEFORE archiving rather than relying on propagation.\n   https://docs.exalate.com/docs/how-to-synchronize-issue-links-on-jira-on-premise\n   https://docs.exalate.com/docs/jira-cloud-issue-link-sync\n   https://community.exalate.com/t/delete-remote-link-when-archiving-an-issue/6775\n\n**READ AGAINST OUR OWN SITUATION.** Two things follow, and they point the same way:\n\n 1. This is NOT a Cloud/DC parity gap either. Verified earlier on this ticket: `compute_inbound_mutations`\n    has ONE call site in backend-agnostic core and both backends implement the same `map_remote_links`\n    port, so Cloud does not mirror peer link deletions and never has. DC is not behind Cloud, and rebar\n    is not behind the field — the field mostly declines to do this on purpose.\n 2. **We now have first-hand evidence for why.** The sibling defect on 88d9 shipped the same inference\n    (peer absence + our own provenance marker = a deletion) and destroyed 63 parent edges on its first\n    production pass. Aha!'s stated rationale is not hypothetical caution; it is precisely our incident.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET: DEFER THE BUILD**, per the operator's rule. Concretely that means rebar's\nshipped semantics for a peer-side link deletion are LOCAL-WINS-AND-RESTORE — the link is re-added on the\nnext pass — which is convergent, loses no local data, is identical on both backends, and is in line with\nmature tools in this space rather than behind them.\n\nWhat the deferral does NOT mean: it does not mean the analysis is discarded. The correct design is known\nand now has a working template — the per-binding peer-state record that change 1296 introduced for\nparents (`get_peer_parent`/`set_peer_parent`, advanced only on OBSERVATION). A link version is the same\nshape and `binding_store` is already a parameter of both link differs, so whoever picks this up starts\nfrom a proven pattern rather than from the refuted `managed_refs` premise.\n\nRecorded so the next reader does not re-litigate it from scratch, and so a green Cloud suite is not\nmistaken for coverage that does not exist (Cloud has NO inbound-link-deletion cell at all — filed\nseparately for the parity epic)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785775717605363001,"uuid":"457d3094-8c25-4ded-990c-1c23b3eb6d77"}